{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the turbulent rise of facebook with precision, intensity, and emotional depth. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a modern tragedy about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached, a man driven more by rivalry and validation than by altruism. his portrayal is chillingly accurate, making zuckerberg both sympathetic and infuriating.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally stellar. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s loyal friend turned adversary, conveying heartbreak and moral conflict with quiet power. jesse plemons and armie hammer bring nuance to their roles as the winklevoss twins, embodying privilege and entitlement with a chilling duality. the film’s pacing is relentless, driven by sorkin’s razor-sharp dialogue that crackles with wit and tension. every line feels deliberate, every courtroom exchange a battle of words and wills.\n\ndanny elfman’s score, though unorthodox for a modern drama, is hauntingly effective—electronic pulses mirror the digital world being built, while the rhythm echoes the relentless march of time and progress. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is cool and clinical, reflecting the sterile, high-tech environments where human connections are increasingly mediated by screens.\n\nwhat elevates the film beyond its subject matter is its exploration of themes: the erosion of friendship in the face of success, the ambiguity of genius, and the paradox of connection in an age of isolation. the social network doesn’t glorify facebook; it dissects it. it asks whether the world we’ve built through technology is truly connecting us—or fragmenting us further.\n\nthe film’s structure, intercutting between two lawsuits and the timeline of facebook’s founding, is a brilliant narrative device that keeps the audience engaged while slowly revealing the emotional core beneath the corporate drama. the final scene, with zuckerberg refreshing his ex-girlfriend’s facebook page, is devastating in its simplicity—a quiet moment that says more than any monologue ever could.\n\nthe social network is not just a movie about a website. it’s a mirror held up to our digital age, and it’s as relevant today as it was in 2010. it’s smart, fast, cold, and beautiful—a perfect storm of writing, acting, and direction that cements it as one of the greatest films of the 21st century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -1.2010457515716553}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -2.361959457397461}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.41402581334114075}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00031668893643654883}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.2032429724931717}, {"token": " precision", "logprob": -0.3965921401977539}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -5.8887653722194955e-05}, {"token": " intensity", "logprob": -1.1499385833740234}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " and", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " emotional", "logprob": -0.398525208234787}, {"token": " depth", 